The `catch` block throws a `RuntimeException`. However, the calling `verify()` method is designed to catch `IOException` and wrap it in a `MojoExecutionException`. Throwing a `RuntimeException` here breaks this pattern and will cause the Maven plugin to terminate with an unhandled exception. It would be better to throw an `IOException` to maintain consistency with the surrounding code's error handling strategy. This also applies to the `catch` block on lines 78-80.

```suggestion
} catch (IOException e) {
    throw new IOException("unable to read file " + file, e);
}
```

![Important](https://app.propelcode.ai/important-priority.svg)

[**Logic**] Currently `santizeAnchors()` only iterates over anchor tags that exist in the translation. If a translator forgets to add the closing `</a>` (or drops the whole link) the `englishMatcher` still has remaining tags, but we never inspect it after the loop, so the broken HTML ships and everything after the `<a>` becomes part of the link. Example: English `Click <a href="{0}">here</a>` vs translation `Kliknij <a href="{0}">tutaj` (missing `</a>`). The opening tag is accepted and removed, the method exits, and the missing closing tag is never reported. After the loop, also assert that `englishMatcher` has no remaining matches, e.g.

```java
while (matcher.find()) { ... }
if (englishMatcher.find()) {
    messages.add("Missing anchor tag " + englishMatcher.group() + " in translation");
}
```
so that missing closing tags or dropped anchors are caught.

