## Code Review - Obvious Bugs Found

I found **2 significant bugs** in this PR that should be addressed:

---

### Bug #1: Potential NullPointerException in `hasPermission(ClientModel, String)` method

**Location:** `services/src/main/java/org/keycloak/services/resources/admin/permissions/ClientPermissionsV2.java`, lines 217-219

**Code:**
```java
resource = AdminPermissionsSchema.SCHEMA.getResourceTypeResource(session, server, AdminPermissionsSchema.CLIENTS_RESOURCE_TYPE);

if (authz.getStoreFactory().getPolicyStore().findByResource(server, resource).isEmpty()) {
    return false;
}
```

**Issue:** The `getResourceTypeResource()` method can return `null` (see `AdminPermissionsSchema.java` lines 116-134). If it returns `null`, the subsequent call to `findByResource(server, resource)` on line 219 will pass a null resource parameter, which could cause a NullPointerException or unexpected behavior.

**What could go wrong:** 
- When checking permissions for a client and no specific resource exists, if the "all-clients" resource also doesn't exist or the authorization schema is not supported, this will pass `null` to `findByResource()`, potentially crashing the permission check.
- This would deny access when it should fail gracefully or return false.

**Fix needed:** Add a null check after line 217:
```java
resource = AdminPermissionsSchema.SCHEMA.getResourceTypeResource(session, server, AdminPermissionsSchema.CLIENTS_RESOURCE_TYPE);

if (resource == null) {
    return false;
}

if (authz.getStoreFactory().getPolicyStore().findByResource(server, resource).isEmpty()) {
    return false;
}
```

---

### Bug #2: Missing CLIENTS resource type handling in `getResourceName()` method

**Location:** `server-spi-private/src/main/java/org/keycloak/authorization/AdminPermissionsSchema.java`, lines 304-326

**Code:**
```java
public String getResourceName(KeycloakSession session, Policy policy, Resource resource) {
    ResourceServer resourceServer = policy.getResourceServer();

    if (supportsAuthorizationSchema(session, resourceServer)) {
        String resourceType = policy.getResourceType();

        if (USERS.getType().equals(resourceType)) {
            if (resource.getName().equals(USERS_RESOURCE_TYPE)) {
                return "All users";
            }

            UserModel user = session.users().getUserById(session.getContext().getRealm(), resource.getName());

            if (user == null) {
                throw new ModelIllegalStateException("User not found for resource [" + resource.getId() + "]");
            }

            return user.getUsername();
        }
    }

    return resource.getDisplayName();
}
```

**Issue:** This method handles the USERS resource type but does not handle the newly added CLIENTS resource type. When policies are created for client resources, this method will fall through to `return resource.getDisplayName()` without properly resolving the client name.

**What could go wrong:**
- When displaying or managing client permissions in the admin UI or API, the resource names won't be properly resolved to human-readable client names.
- The method will return raw internal IDs instead of meaningful client names or "All clients" for the all-clients resource.
- This creates an inconsistency - users get nice names, but clients don't.

**Fix needed:** Add handling for CLIENTS resource type similar to USERS:
```java
public String getResourceName(KeycloakSession session, Policy policy, Resource resource) {
    ResourceServer resourceServer = policy.getResourceServer();

    if (supportsAuthorizationSchema(session, resourceServer)) {
        String resourceType = policy.getResourceType();

        if (USERS.getType().equals(resourceType)) {
            if (resource.getName().equals(USERS_RESOURCE_TYPE)) {
                return "All users";
            }

            UserModel user = session.users().getUserById(session.getContext().getRealm(), resource.getName());

            if (user == null) {
                throw new ModelIllegalStateException("User not found for resource [" + resource.getId() + "]");
            }

            return user.getUsername();
        } else if (CLIENTS.getType().equals(resourceType)) {
            if (resource.getName().equals(CLIENTS_RESOURCE_TYPE)) {
                return "All clients";
            }

            ClientModel client = session.clients().getClientById(session.getContext().getRealm(), resource.getName());

            if (client == null) {
                throw new ModelIllegalStateException("Client not found for resource [" + resource.getId() + "]");
            }

            return client.getClientId();
        }
    }

    return resource.getDisplayName();
}
```

---

