**Unconditional DB call introduces a performance regression**

`idpDelegate.getByAlias(alias)` is now called unconditionally on every `remove()`, even when the cache is warm and valid. Before this PR it was only called in the `isInvalid(cacheKey)` branch (cache miss/invalidated path). The else branch already had access to the IDP's model through the cached entry (`cached.getIdentityProvider()`), so the extra DB round-trip is unnecessary when the cache is live.

A correct fix would read the IDP from the cache when available and only fall back to the delegate when the cache is invalid or cold:

```java
IdentityProviderModel storedIdp;
if (isInvalid(cacheKey)) {
    storedIdp = idpDelegate.getByAlias(alias);
    registerIDPInvalidation(storedIdp);
} else {
    CachedIdentityProvider cached = realmCache.getCache().get(cacheKey, CachedIdentityProvider.class);
    if (cached != null) {
        storedIdp = cached.getIdentityProvider();
        registerIDPInvalidation(storedIdp);
    } else {
        storedIdp = idpDelegate.getByAlias(alias);
    }
}
registerCountInvalidation();
registerIDPLoginInvalidation(storedIdp);
return idpDelegate.remove(alias);
```

This keeps the same behaviour for `registerIDPInvalidation` while avoiding the extra DB query on the cache-hit path.

