`isProvisionedPromRule` is declared to take a `PromRuleDTO`, but it checks `promRule.provenance` even though `PromRuleDTO` does not declare a `provenance` field. This relies on structural compatibility with `GrafanaPromRuleDTO` and will fail TypeScript type-checking; it also makes the function misleading for non-Grafana rules. Consider tightening the parameter type to `GrafanaPromRuleDTO` (or another type that explicitly includes `provenance`) and/or using a type guard to narrow to Grafana-managed rules before reading `provenance`.
```suggestion
function hasProvenance(
  promRule: PromRuleDTO
): promRule is PromRuleDTO & { provenance?: unknown } {
  return 'provenance' in (promRule as object);
}

export function isProvisionedPromRule(promRule: PromRuleDTO): boolean {
  return (
    prometheusRuleType.grafana.rule(promRule) &&
    hasProvenance(promRule) &&
    Boolean(promRule.provenance)
  );
```

