`-webkit-box-ordinal-group vs order property CSS flexbox 1-based 0-based value mapping`

💡 Result:

`-webkit-box-ordinal-group` is from the **2009 flexbox draft** (`display: -webkit-box`) and uses **1-based “ordinal groups”**:

- **Allowed values:** positive integers only (natural numbers)  
- **Default:** `1` ([w3.org](https://www.w3.org/TR/2009/WD-css3-flexbox-20090723/?utm_source=openai))

Modern Flexbox uses `order` (on `display: flex` items):

- **Allowed values:** any integer (can be negative, zero, positive)  
- **Default:** `0` ([developer.mozilla.org](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Reference/Properties/order?utm_source=openai))

### Value mapping (1-based ↔ 0-based)
To keep the *same visual ordering* when migrating between the two:

- **From old to new:**  
  `order = (-webkit-box-ordinal-group) - 1` ([w3.org](https://www.w3.org/TR/2009/WD-css3-flexbox-20090723/?utm_source=openai))
- **From new to old:**  
  `-webkit-box-ordinal-group = order + 1` ([w3.org](https://www.w3.org/TR/2009/WD-css3-flexbox-20090723/?utm_source=openai))

### Important limitation
Because `-webkit-box-ordinal-group` must be **≥ 1**, you **cannot exactly represent** modern values like `order: 0`? (you can: it maps to `-webkit-box-ordinal-group: 1`) but you **cannot represent negative `order`** (it would require `-webkit-box-ordinal-group <= 0`, which isn’t allowed). ([developer.mozilla.org](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/box-ordinal-group?utm_source=openai))

