There's a potential memory leak here. `URL.createObjectURL` creates a URL that needs to be explicitly released by calling `URL.revokeObjectURL`. While you are revoking the old URL before creating a new one, the last created URL is never revoked when the component unmounts.

To fix this, you should use a `useEffect` hook to manage the lifecycle of the object URL and revoke it in the cleanup function. For example:

```tsx
useEffect(() => {
  // This will be run when the component unmounts.
  return () => {
    if (backupCodesUrl) {
      URL.revokeObjectURL(backupCodesUrl);
    }
  };
}, [backupCodesUrl]);
```
You should add this effect to your component.

