Summary
- This PR switches the app-store to use dynamic imports (appStore entries are Promises) and updates consumers to await appStore[...] and getCalendar(...) which is now async.
- Good: it modernises lazy-loading of app packages so unused apps aren't loaded eagerly.
- Risks / issues found that need attention (actionable items below).

High-level issues and recommended fixes

1) Un-awaited async callbacks inside forEach (lost errors and race conditions)
- Problem: Several places use .forEach(async (item) => { ... await ... }); but forEach does not await the callbacks — the parent function continues and try/catch won't catch errors from those async callbacks. Examples from the diff / repo:
  - packages/app-store/vital/lib/reschedule.ts: bookingRefsFiltered.forEach(async (bookingRef) => { ... })
  - packages/app-store/wipemycalother/lib/reschedule.ts: same
  - packages/trpc/server/routers/viewer/bookings.tsx and features/.../handleCancelBooking.ts: multiple forEach(async ...)
- Why it matters: async work may be fired but not awaited; errors will be uncaught, and subsequent code may run before integrations finish (e.g. deleting calendar events).
- Fix: Either use for...of + await inside, or collect promises with map and await Promise.all.
  - Example replace:
    bookingRefsFiltered.forEach(async (bookingRef) => { /* ... */ });
  - With (await all in parallel):
    const promises = bookingRefsFiltered.map((bookingRef) => {
      if (!bookingRef.uid) return Promise.resolve();
      if (bookingRef.type.endsWith("_calendar")) {
        return getCalendar(...).then(calendar => calendar?.deleteEvent(...));
      }
      return deleteMeeting(...);
    });
    await Promise.all(promises);
  - Or sequential:
    for (const bookingRef of bookingRefsFiltered) {
      if (!bookingRef.uid) continue;
      if (bookingRef.type.endsWith("_calendar")) {
        const calendar = await getCalendar(...);
        await calendar?.deleteEvent(...);
      } else {
        await deleteMeeting(...);
      }
    }

2) Sequential awaits where parallel could be used (performance)
- Example: packages/core/videoClient.ts getVideoAdapters loops sequentially awaiting each app import. When multiple credentials are present, this loads apps serially.
- Suggestion: load app imports in parallel using Promise.all and then filter:
  const entries = withCredentials.map(cred => {
    const appName = cred.type.split("_").join("");
    return appStore[appName as keyof typeof appStore]; // returns Promise<module>
  });
  const loaded = await Promise.all(entries);
  // then process loaded modules
- Similarly, when resolving many calendars in getCachedResults (CalendarManager), the code already awaits Promise.all for calendars — good. But other spots that await inside loops could use parallel fetches (when safe).

3) Type system and runtime assumptions: appStore entries are Promises
- The index.ts now sets values like applecalendar: import("./applecalendar"), so appStore[...] returns a Promise<module>.
- The codebase updated many places to await appStore[...] (good) but ensure typings reflect Promise types:
  - appStore currently typed as an object of Promises, but some code uses keyof typeof appStore. Make sure TypeScript types reflect that values are Promise<R> and callers expect Awaited<typeof appStore[...]>.
  - getCalendar has been converted to async and most call sites updated. Verify there are no remaining call sites expecting synchronous Calendar instance.
- Also ensure tests and any runtime code that uses appStore directly (without awaiting) are updated.

4) try/catch around forEach(async ...) — ineffective
- In several places the code wraps a forEach(async ...) in try/catch expecting to catch errors, but the try/catch won't catch errors thrown asynchronously inside the forEach callbacks. Convert to awaited Promise.all or for..of + await inside the try block so catch works.

5) Returning values from forEach(async) callbacks
- Several loops used return inside forEach(async ...) but those returns only exit the callback, not the outer function. If the intention was to short-circuit, this doesn't work. Use for...of + return, or use some control flow with Promise.race/Array.some etc., depending on intention.

6) Inconsistent handling of non-existing apps
- The code checks if (app && "lib" in app && "CalendarService" in app.lib) — when app is a module Promise that on await resolves to a module object, this is fine after awaiting. But remember import(...) resolves to the module namespace. If app libraries export default / named exports differently than expected, make sure the module shape matches assumptions.
- Also some error messages log `payment App service of type ${paymentApp}` — now paymentApp is a module object not a string; consider logging paymentAppCredential?.app?.dirName instead for clarity.

7) Potential increased latency and race conditions when awaiting getCalendar inside loops that previously were synchronous
- Prior code got calendars synchronously and then called deleteEvent in parallel. Now awaiting inside loop could make operations serial. Where appropriate, gather calendar promises first and then call deleteEvent in parallel.

Concrete examples / patches to apply

- Replace forEach(async ...) patterns. Example change (pattern found in multiple files):

Before:
try {
  bookingRefsFiltered.forEach(async (bookingRef) => {
    if (bookingRef.uid) {
      if (bookingRef.type.endsWith("_calendar")) {
        const calendar = await getCalendar(credentialsMap.get(bookingRef.type));
        return calendar?.deleteEvent(bookingRef.uid, builder.calendarEvent);
      } else if (bookingRef.type.endsWith("_video")) {
        return deleteMeeting(credentialsMap.get(bookingRef.type), bookingRef.uid);
      }
    }
  });
} catch (error) { ... }

After (parallel):
try {
  const promises = bookingRefsFiltered.map((bookingRef) => {
    if (!bookingRef.uid) return Promise.resolve();
    if (bookingRef.type.endsWith("_calendar")) {
      return getCalendar(credentialsMap.get(bookingRef.type)).then((calendar) =>
        calendar?.deleteEvent(bookingRef.uid, builder.calendarEvent)
      );
    } else if (bookingRef.type.endsWith("_video")) {
      return deleteMeeting(credentialsMap.get(bookingRef.type), bookingRef.uid);
    }
    return Promise.resolve();
  });
  await Promise.all(promises);
} catch (error) { ... }

Or sequential if order/consistency required:
try {
  for (const bookingRef of bookingRefsFiltered) {
    if (!bookingRef.uid) continue;
    if (bookingRef.type.endsWith("_calendar")) {
      const calendar = await getCalendar(credentialsMap.get(bookingRef.type));
      await calendar?.deleteEvent(bookingRef.uid, builder.calendarEvent);
    } else if (bookingRef.type.endsWith("_video")) {
      await deleteMeeting(credentialsMap.get(bookingRef.type), bookingRef.uid);
    }
  }
} catch (error) { ... }

- Improve getVideoAdapters to load apps in parallel (performance):
Before:
const videoAdapters: VideoApiAdapter[] = [];
for (const cred of withCredentials) {
  const appName = ...
  const app = await appStore[appName];
  if (app && "lib" in app && "VideoApiAdapter" in app.lib) {
